d" commit_message="Collect and update lab info.  BUG=None TEST=None  Change-Id: I3c16ffe58d89d43f7055f3a9f260a2376212d1e7 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/321864 Commit-Ready: danny chan &lt;dchan@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: danny chan &lt;dchan@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Wai-Hong Tam &lt;waihong@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="321864" owner_email="dchan@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/third_party/autotest" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/autotest" ref="refs/changes/64/321864/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="I2393f4c9fa70d05751b62c0968288030dc26d0c8" commit="fb33cb4155d3360b51fe2c9c7f3db2854d562961" commit_message="UPSTREAM: include/linux/poison.h: fix LIST_POISON{1,2} offset  Poison pointer values should be small enough to find a room in non-mmap'able/hardly-mmap'able space.  E.g.  on x86 &quot;poison pointer space&quot; is located starting from 0x0.  Given unprivileged users cannot mmap anything below mmap_min_addr, it should be safe to use poison pointers lower than mmap_min_addr.  The current poison pointer values of LIST_POISON{1,2} might be too big for mmap_min_addr values equal or less than 1 MB (common case, e.g.  Ubuntu uses only 0x10000).  There is little point to use such a big value given the &quot;poison pointer space&quot; below 1 MB is not yet exhausted.  Changing it to a smaller value solves the problem for small mmap_min_addr setups.  The values are suggested by Solar Designer: http://www.openwall.com/lists/oss-security/2015/05/02/6  BUG=b:26186802 TEST=smaug compiles  Signed-off-by: Vasily Kulikov &lt;segoon@openwall.com&gt; Cc: Solar Designer &lt;solar@openwall.com&gt; Cc: Thomas Gleixner &lt;tglx@linutronix.de&gt; Cc: &quot;Kirill A. Shutemov&quot; &lt;kirill.shutemov@linux.intel.com&gt; Signed-off-by: Andrew Morton &lt;akpm@linux-foundation.org&gt; Signed-off-by: Linus Torvalds &lt;torvalds@linux-foundation.org&gt; (cherry picked from commit 8a5e5e02fc83aaf67053ab53b359af08c6c49aaf) Signed-off-by: David Riley &lt;davidriley@chromium.org&gt;  Change-Id: I2393f4c9fa70d05751b62c0968288030dc26d0c8 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/323336 Commit-Ready: David Riley &lt;davidriley@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: David Riley &lt;davidriley@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Andrew Bresticker &lt;abrestic@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="323336" owner_email="davidriley@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/36/323336/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I11aabde07c6f852a5b8c53a97ad0892c3e0e4d30" commit="76814fb9f46abc684387e948fe19a1046a05e955" commit_message="smbios: Factor out common DIMM speed and count functions  This places copy+pasted functions for obtaining DIMM count and speed via SMBIOS into a central location.  BUG=none BRANCH=none TEST=compiles for x86  Signed-off-by: David Hendricks &lt;dhendrix@chromium.org&gt; Change-Id: I11aabde07c6f852a5b8c53a97ad0892c3e0e4d30 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/319585 Reviewed-by: Duncan Laurie &lt;dlaurie@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="319585" owner_email="dhendrix@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/platform/mosys" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/mosys" ref="refs/changes/85/319585/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><lkgm version="7855.0.0-rc4"/></manifest>